    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense but what about the downsides? like i hear a lot about how these deals basically kill off local manufacturing in certain countries and make everyone too dependent on other nations for stuff they actually need. is that just a bad argument or is there some truth to it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "There is definite truth to the concerns regarding the \"downside,\" often related to **uneven distribution of benefits and increased domestic competition for certain industries**. When a powerful, highly efficient global competitor from a more developed nation undercuts a less efficient local firm, that local \"uncompetitive\" industry can indeed decline or \"die off,\" leading to local job losses.\n\nThis is the argument that **vulnerability and over-reliance** are real political and social concerns, as a sudden global supply chain shock can deeply impact the well-being of a nation that has specialized too heavily in one or two globally dependent areas.\n\nHowever, proponents argue this should lead to **re-skilling and the development of entirely new, more competitive local sectors,** which is the necessary long-term political and social challenge that policy must address.",
      "source": "target",
